WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.730
In the famous Disney film, Pocahontas sang
about the “Colors of the Wind”, and we

00:00:03.730 --> 00:00:07.600
learned that everyone can get along, despite
their cultural differences.

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:12.829
While the intent behind the movie may have
been good, it is nowhere near being historically

00:00:12.829 --> 00:00:13.829
accurate.

00:00:13.829 --> 00:00:18.210
The true story is much darker, and for some
of you out there, this video just may ruin

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:19.360
your childhood memories.

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:26.310
This is the true story of Pocahontas.

00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:37.880
A Peaceful Existence
The first thing you should know about Pocahontas

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:41.040
is that it wasn’t her birth name.

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:45.079
Native Americans have a unique tradition when
it comes to someone’s name and identity.

00:00:45.079 --> 00:00:48.201
Children are given a name at birth, and it
often changes as the person grows up, and

00:00:48.201 --> 00:00:50.530
the family learns more about their personality.

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:51.840
At birth, her name was “Amonute”.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:56.630
And as as she grew older, she was developing
into a true beauty, so her father gave her

00:00:56.630 --> 00:01:00.620
the new name Matoaka, which means “The flower
between two streams”.

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:04.500
She was apart of the The Powhatan tribe of
Eastern Virginia, which had a population of

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:05.740
over 25,000 people.

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:10.360
They were broken up into roughly 30 smaller
tribes, which were governed by respected village

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:12.070
leaders.

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:19.930
Pocahontas’ father, Wahunsenacawh, was the
one head chief who was above all other tribal

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:23.060
leaders, known as the honorable “Chief Powhatan”.

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:26.950
The Powhatan people had a tradition where
one woman from each tribe was sent to become

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:29.670
one of the wives of the Chief Powhatan.

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:33.140
Women came of age at 14 years old, and they
often found a husband soon after.

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:36.479
So it’s likely that they would also be around
this age when they were chosen.

00:01:36.479 --> 00:01:40.380
Once the young woman gave birth to his baby
and weaned them, she could leave the child

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:44.710
to be raised by the Chief, and go back to
her home village to get married and started

00:01:44.710 --> 00:01:45.710
a family of her own.

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:49.189
While it sounds like a frightening prospect
for the modern world, this was considered

00:01:49.189 --> 00:01:50.440
to be a huge honor.

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:54.060
Only the most beautiful and intelligent girls
were chosen to have children with the Chief,

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:57.310
so that they could pass on the best traits
of the tribe together.

00:01:57.310 --> 00:02:00.550
These children of the Chief were given special
training, and often grew up to be the next

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:02.159
generation of leaders in their community.

00:02:02.159 --> 00:02:06.000
There was one wife who held a special place
in Chief Wahunsenacawh heart.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.450
He gave her the nickname “Pocahontas”,
which means “the playful one”.

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:13.330
When he was raising her daughter, Amonute,
he noticed that the girl’s personality was

00:02:13.330 --> 00:02:15.099
almost exactly like her mother.

00:02:15.099 --> 00:02:18.570
He started to call her “Pocahontas” as
well, because they were so much alike.

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:22.540
There is no historic record of what happened
to her mother, but most historians believe

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:25.060
that she must have died during childbirth.

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:29.190
As a young girl, Matoaka would run through
the forest, climb trees, and do cartwheels.

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:33.750
She was always laughing and smiling, ready
and willing to play with her dozens of half-siblings.

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:38.980
Her mother’s name truly did suit her, so
at age 14, when she went through her coming-of-age

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:41.921
ceremony, she chose to officially change her
name to Pocahontas.

00:02:41.921 --> 00:02:51.140
Colonization, and The Lies of John Smith
In the early 1600’s crews of British explorers

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:54.450
gathered supplies on their ships, and sailed
towards the New World.

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:57.709
Once they arrived, they founded the Jamestown
Colony in 1607.

00:02:57.709 --> 00:03:01.750
One of these explorers was a man named John
Smith, who was dubbed the “President of

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:04.150
Virginia” and the “Admiral of New England”.

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:08.519
He would later publish a book bragging about
his adventures, which many modern-day historians

00:03:08.519 --> 00:03:09.660
know is full of fiction.

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:14.030
In his book, John Smith claims that soon after
he arrived in Virginia, he was captured by

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:17.880
a man named Opechancanough, and he was tied
up and paraded around all 30 villages.

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:21.670
Then, he was brought to Chief Wahunsenacawh,
who ordered his execution.

00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:25.970
Apparently, he accepted his fate with dignity,
but just as his head was about to be smashed

00:03:25.970 --> 00:03:30.540
on a rock, a beautiful girl named Pocahontas
comes to his rescue, begging her father for

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:31.540
mercy.

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:37.010
According to him, she said that if he must
kill John Smith, then she should die, too.

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:39.410
He goes on to talk about their newfound friendship.

00:03:39.410 --> 00:03:41.460
Kind of sounds like the Disney movie, right?

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:46.540
But most historians agree that this sounds
completely ridiculous, for so many reasons.

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:50.129
First of all, Pocahontas would have only been
9 or 10 years old at the time, and John Smith

00:03:50.129 --> 00:03:51.349
was 27.

00:03:51.349 --> 00:03:54.930
Children were never allowed to be near anything
as brutal as a trial or execution.

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:59.459
This story also paints the Native Americans
as being savage, and willing to kill anyone

00:03:59.459 --> 00:04:00.830
without a valid reason.

00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:05.159
The true story is that in 1608, the Jamestown
colonists were starving, because they had

00:04:05.159 --> 00:04:06.650
run out of supplies.

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:10.720
Instead of trying to negotiate a fair trade,
John Smith ordered his men to attack the tribal

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:13.049
villages, and steal whatever they could.

00:04:13.049 --> 00:04:16.430
He would also set fires, and demanded that
they give in to his demands.

00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:20.519
However, the weakened colonists were no match
for the tribe, so many of the white men were

00:04:20.519 --> 00:04:21.919
killed or taken prisoner.

00:04:21.919 --> 00:04:25.840
John Smith was their leader, and he begged
for the Chief to spare his life.

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:30.360
Chief Powhatan chose to have mercy on John
Smith, without Pocahontas being involved at

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:31.360
all.

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:34.069
Later, John Smith would have spotted the beautiful
little girl playing with her friends.

00:04:34.069 --> 00:04:37.150
When he found out that Pocahontas was the
Chief’s favorite daughter, he made a mental

00:04:37.150 --> 00:04:41.110
note of it, and filed that away for later.

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:47.969
An Act of War
When she was 14 or 15 years old, Pocahontas

00:04:47.969 --> 00:04:52.029
married a young man named Kocoum, who was
the brother of one of the village leaders

00:04:52.029 --> 00:04:53.470
known as Japasaw.

00:04:53.470 --> 00:04:57.099
She moved to this new place to be with her
husband, and this new village was much closer

00:04:57.099 --> 00:04:58.969
to Jamestown than where she grew up.

00:04:58.969 --> 00:05:03.009
And the news of this new “princess” moving
into the tribe’s village spread, so even

00:05:03.009 --> 00:05:05.729
the colonists knew of Pocahontas’ arrival.

00:05:05.729 --> 00:05:09.509
John Smith also remembered from years before
that she was the Chief’s favorite.

00:05:09.509 --> 00:05:13.979
In the summertime, the Native Americans would
walk around naked when it got too hot outside.

00:05:13.979 --> 00:05:17.749
To them, there was nothing sexual or exploitive
about it- they were all just people trying

00:05:17.749 --> 00:05:18.749
to keep cool.

00:05:18.749 --> 00:05:21.270
But to the English settlers in Jamestown,
this was shocking.

00:05:21.270 --> 00:05:26.009
And considering that many of these were men
who already saw the natives as less-than-human,

00:05:26.009 --> 00:05:29.680
and they hadn’t been with a woman in a very
long time...

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:31.039
You can guess what happened.

00:05:31.039 --> 00:05:34.789
Women and children were taken from their homes
and violated.

00:05:34.789 --> 00:05:39.469
In Powhatan culture, if a man committed sexual
assault, the rapist was immediately sentenced

00:05:39.469 --> 00:05:40.469
to death.

00:05:40.469 --> 00:05:42.759
As soon as they see that someone is a rapist
or a murderer, they’re gone.

00:05:42.759 --> 00:05:44.499
There is no such thing as rehabilitation.

00:05:44.499 --> 00:05:46.490
They’re never going to pass on their DNA.

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:47.490
Sorted.

00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:50.229
This made the crime extremely rare.

00:05:50.229 --> 00:05:52.879
In England, though, men got away with rape
all the time.

00:05:52.879 --> 00:05:56.289
Women were often accused of lying, unless
there was a witness- which, of course, there

00:05:56.289 --> 00:05:57.479
almost never was.

00:05:57.479 --> 00:06:00.960
Even if he was caught, the punishments were
far from a death sentence.

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:04.449
So when the Powhatans people interpreted this
as an act of war, and the Chief Powhatan was

00:06:04.449 --> 00:06:05.789
ready to attack Jamestown.

00:06:05.789 --> 00:06:08.869
This began the first Anglo-Pawhatan War.

00:06:08.869 --> 00:06:11.960
Meanwhile, Pocahontas was now 16 years old,
and heavily pregnant.

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:15.599
The white men in Jamestown devised a plan
that they would kidnap her and use her as

00:06:15.599 --> 00:06:19.349
leverage, to stop Chief Powhatan from completely
obliterating the colony.

00:06:19.349 --> 00:06:21.899
Pocahontas had just given birth to her first
baby.

00:06:21.899 --> 00:06:25.379
She would have been in the care of the tribal
women, and away from her husband, Kocoum.

00:06:25.379 --> 00:06:30.099
Soon after delivering her child, a white man
named Captain Samuel Argall snuck into Pocahontas’

00:06:30.099 --> 00:06:32.550
tent in the middle of the night, and kidnapped
her.

00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:36.409
Physically weak and recovering from giving
birth, Pocahontas didn’t stand a chance

00:06:36.409 --> 00:06:37.929
at defending herself.

00:06:37.929 --> 00:06:41.719
In his book, John Smith described the scene
of Pocahontas being carried to the English

00:06:41.719 --> 00:06:42.789
men’s massive ship.

00:06:42.789 --> 00:06:46.879
She had never seen this technology before,
so she was screaming in horror as her abductors

00:06:46.879 --> 00:06:47.970
forced her inside.

00:06:47.970 --> 00:06:51.860
There was a jail cell in the bottom of Captain
Argall’s boat, which is where they locked

00:06:51.860 --> 00:06:52.860
her up.

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:56.129
In his version of the story, John Smith blamed
everything on the Native Americans, of course.

00:06:56.129 --> 00:07:00.009
He wrote that the Powhatan had stolen their
weapons and supplies from Jamestown, and they

00:07:00.009 --> 00:07:03.860
were using Pocahontas as leverage to broker
a peace treaty.

00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:07.830
Even though he writes about her distress,
he never goes into further detail about what

00:07:07.830 --> 00:07:08.830
happened to her.

00:07:08.830 --> 00:07:12.849
According to the Native American’s oral
history, Pocahontas’ husband, Kocoum heard

00:07:12.849 --> 00:07:14.669
the news that she had been kidnapped.

00:07:14.669 --> 00:07:18.669
Kocoum galloped towards Jamestown the next
day, killing men with his arrows as he went.

00:07:18.669 --> 00:07:22.929
He made it all the way to Captain Argall’s
ship, but he was gunned down before he could

00:07:22.929 --> 00:07:23.929
save her.

00:07:23.929 --> 00:07:28.059
John Smith wrote that they kept Pocahontas
while they waited for payment from Chief Powhatan.

00:07:28.059 --> 00:07:33.120
He says, “We received part of the payment,
and returned (her father) this answer: That

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:37.479
his daughter should be well used; but we could
not believe the rest of our arms were either

00:07:37.479 --> 00:07:42.409
lost or stolen from him, and therefore til
he sent them, we would keep his daughter.”

00:07:42.409 --> 00:07:47.139
So...Basically, this guy is justifying kidnapping
a teenage girl and blackmailing her father,

00:07:47.139 --> 00:07:50.779
and somehow, the English thought this was
totally acceptable when they read it in his

00:07:50.779 --> 00:07:51.779
book.

00:07:51.779 --> 00:07:54.979
Of course, those weapons never came, because
they had never been stolen in the first place.

00:07:54.979 --> 00:07:57.400
Pocahontas eventually fell into a state of
depression.

00:07:57.400 --> 00:08:01.169
We know from the Native American oral history
that she stopped eating, because she missed

00:08:01.169 --> 00:08:02.449
her family so much.

00:08:02.449 --> 00:08:06.409
The colonists needed to keep her alive for
leverage, so her sister was finally allowed

00:08:06.409 --> 00:08:07.409
to visit.

00:08:07.409 --> 00:08:10.909
When the two young women were allowed to speak,
Pocahontas told her that she was being raped

00:08:10.909 --> 00:08:13.090
by the crew of the ship on a regular basis.

00:08:13.090 --> 00:08:17.269
She begged to come home, but the English men
were refusing to let her leave, unless her

00:08:17.269 --> 00:08:20.460
father promised to give them weapons and supplies,
and sign a peace treaty.

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:26.539
But of course, Chief Wahunsenacawh was never
going to stop the war until he got his daughter

00:08:26.539 --> 00:08:27.539
back.

00:08:27.539 --> 00:08:31.679
Don’t Worry, it Gets Worse.

00:08:31.679 --> 00:08:36.690
Records show that the First Anglo-Powhatan
War lasted from 1610 to 1614, but the timeline

00:08:36.690 --> 00:08:38.330
of events gets a bit blurry.

00:08:38.330 --> 00:08:41.529
According to John Smith, Pocahontas was only
on the ship for three months.

00:08:41.529 --> 00:08:44.949
But if you actually think about it logically,
she must have been kept prisoner on that boat

00:08:44.949 --> 00:08:47.860
for at least a year, if not longer.

00:08:47.860 --> 00:08:51.620
Some records claim that she was kidnapped
near the beginning of the war, in 1610, while

00:08:51.620 --> 00:08:54.000
others say it happened in 1613.

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:58.440
This is also why many history books are fuzzy
about her exact age and date of birth, because

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:02.040
there are so many conflicting stories of exactly
what happened.

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:05.620
What we do know for certain is that during
her time as a prisoner, Pocahontas became

00:09:05.620 --> 00:09:07.279
pregnant by one of the men.

00:09:07.279 --> 00:09:10.990
Now that there was a child on the way, the
men could no longer hide the fact that she

00:09:10.990 --> 00:09:12.480
had been raped in captivity.

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:15.640
It would seem that there was no hope in negotiating
a peace treaty, now.

00:09:15.640 --> 00:09:18.880
They needed to come up with a plan to cover
up their crimes.

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:20.880
This is where John Rolfe enters the picture.

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:25.230
He is credited for being the first man to
bring tobacco farming to the Jamestown colony.

00:09:25.230 --> 00:09:29.540
He was able to build a sustainable business
for the colonists to make a profit by growing

00:09:29.540 --> 00:09:31.330
tobacco and shipping it to England.

00:09:31.330 --> 00:09:35.070
Since there was so much land in this New World,
tobacco became a booming business.

00:09:35.070 --> 00:09:39.130
So, if you don’t already hate his guts by
the end of this story, you can basically blame

00:09:39.130 --> 00:09:43.610
him for bringing bad smoking habits and lung
cancer to the western world.

00:09:43.610 --> 00:09:45.129
Rolfe was a widower in his 40’s.

00:09:45.129 --> 00:09:49.600
He was friends with all of these men who were
keeping Pocahontas prisoner, and he may have

00:09:49.600 --> 00:09:50.750
been one of the rapists.

00:09:50.750 --> 00:09:53.690
He offered to take one for the team and marry
her.

00:09:53.690 --> 00:09:57.199
The only trouble was that an English person
could not marry a Native American, because

00:09:57.199 --> 00:09:58.459
they were considered less-than-human.

00:09:58.459 --> 00:10:02.579
In 1914, Pocahontas was forced to go through
a Christian Baptism, because it was the one

00:10:02.579 --> 00:10:06.389
and only way they would even consider that
she might have a human soul.

00:10:06.389 --> 00:10:11.120
She was Baptised in the Anglican Church, and
given a new name English name; Rebecca.

00:10:11.120 --> 00:10:14.380
Shortly after her Baptism, she was forced
marry John Rolfe.

00:10:14.380 --> 00:10:17.670
Now that Rolfe and Pocahontas were married,
this sealed the deal.

00:10:17.670 --> 00:10:20.540
The colonists claimed that the two had fallen
in love.

00:10:20.540 --> 00:10:24.170
Once Chief Wahunsenacawh knew that his daughter
was safe, and apparently happy, the peace

00:10:24.170 --> 00:10:26.220
treaty was finally negotiated.

00:10:26.220 --> 00:10:30.459
This became known as the “Peace of Pocahontas”,
because from that day onward, the Jamestown

00:10:30.459 --> 00:10:35.610
colonists and the Powhatan tribe were finally
able to stop fighting, if only for a few years.

00:10:35.610 --> 00:10:39.550
Even though her father had agree to the peace
treaty in hopes to get her back, she was never

00:10:39.550 --> 00:10:42.610
allowed to see her family again.

00:10:42.610 --> 00:10:49.870
Life in England
Once she was married to John Rolfe, Pocahontas

00:10:49.870 --> 00:10:52.209
was forced to begin a new life in England.

00:10:52.209 --> 00:10:54.250
The Powhatan people had a tradition of tattooing
their bodies.

00:10:54.250 --> 00:10:58.519
Even the women had face tattoos, which has
lead some people to believe that Pocahontas

00:10:58.519 --> 00:10:59.660
may have had them, as well.

00:10:59.660 --> 00:11:04.060
But since women were so covered up in England
back then, it may have been easy to hide her

00:11:04.060 --> 00:11:05.060
ink.

00:11:05.060 --> 00:11:07.699
From the perspective of the English people,
this was a wonderful love story.

00:11:07.699 --> 00:11:12.029
Two people from vastly different cultures
were able to fall in love in the New World,

00:11:12.029 --> 00:11:13.550
where anything can happen.

00:11:13.550 --> 00:11:16.009
But of course, not everyone was drinking the
kool aid.

00:11:16.009 --> 00:11:19.230
During an interview, someone asked John Rolfe
if he truly loved her.

00:11:19.230 --> 00:11:22.949
Normally, if someone asks you to prove your
love for your significant other, maybe you’d

00:11:22.949 --> 00:11:25.130
start gushing about all of their wonderful
qualities.

00:11:25.130 --> 00:11:29.940
But John Rolfe responded that if he was actually
trying to take advantage of a teenager, he

00:11:29.940 --> 00:11:33.740
would have chosen a white girl who was “more
pleasing to the eye”.

00:11:33.740 --> 00:11:36.550
Despite all of the glaringly obvious issues
with a 40-something year old man marrying

00:11:36.550 --> 00:11:42.079
a 17 year old, this narrative that Pocahontas
was “choosing” a white husband and converting

00:11:42.079 --> 00:11:46.959
Christianity was also a sign to white people
that maybe these natives weren’t so savage,

00:11:46.959 --> 00:11:47.959
after all.

00:11:47.959 --> 00:11:51.459
They became so popular, people wanted to make
souvenirs of their union.

00:11:51.459 --> 00:11:54.819
Portraits were even painted and sold of Pocahontas
and John Rolfe.

00:11:54.819 --> 00:11:59.019
These happy stories became so popular in English
culture, that their fictional romance was

00:11:59.019 --> 00:12:03.170
published in newspapers everywhere, and it
even inspired the Disney movie Pocahontas

00:12:03.170 --> 00:12:04.170
2.

00:12:04.170 --> 00:12:06.160
John Rolfe claimed that the unborn child was
his, of course.

00:12:06.160 --> 00:12:09.629
And when Pocahontas gave birth to a son, who
they named him Thomas Rolfe.

00:12:09.629 --> 00:12:11.560
He was also baptised as a Christian.

00:12:11.560 --> 00:12:15.589
Legally speaking, John Rolfe was taking on
all of the responsibility as a father.

00:12:15.589 --> 00:12:20.499
He signed his name on both their marriage
certificate, as well as Thomas’ birth certificate.

00:12:20.499 --> 00:12:24.460
John Rolfe got to enjoy Pocahontas’ newfound
fame and popularity, as she was invited to

00:12:24.460 --> 00:12:27.529
the best parties in the country, and became
a household name.

00:12:27.529 --> 00:12:30.699
Portraits were commissioned of her wearing
the traditional European clothing, and she

00:12:30.699 --> 00:12:32.610
had to learn to speak fluent English.

00:12:32.610 --> 00:12:37.120
She was forced to style her hair and makeup
like English women, and completely conform

00:12:37.120 --> 00:12:38.519
to their society.

00:12:38.519 --> 00:12:42.019
Everyone now called her “Rebecca Rolfe”,
instead of her chosen name.

00:12:42.019 --> 00:12:45.460
Despite the fact that she was a young woman
who had been abused and taken from her home,

00:12:45.460 --> 00:12:48.920
Pocahontas handled her new duty as a diplomat
with grace.

00:12:48.920 --> 00:12:53.370
She was representing the Powhatan people,
and everyone called her the “Indian Princess”.

00:12:53.370 --> 00:12:57.420
Pocahontas was incredibly polite to all of
these new English people, and represented

00:12:57.420 --> 00:12:59.430
her tribe to the best of her ability.

00:12:59.430 --> 00:13:03.999
After meeting Queen Anne and King James, their
view of Native Americans completely changed.

00:13:03.999 --> 00:13:07.800
They could see that these people actually
did have souls, and began to push for a more

00:13:07.800 --> 00:13:12.189
peaceful coexistence between the colonists
of the New World, and the natives.

00:13:12.189 --> 00:13:16.199
A few months after arriving in England, John
Smith finally stopped by for a visit.

00:13:16.199 --> 00:13:19.779
By now, Pocahontas had learned of all the
fictional stories he had written about having

00:13:19.779 --> 00:13:21.060
a friendship with her to the Queen.

00:13:21.060 --> 00:13:24.579
In fact, she had thought John Smith was dead,
after he was arrested for what he had done

00:13:24.579 --> 00:13:25.579
to their villages.

00:13:25.579 --> 00:13:29.670
She had no idea that he had used her name
to create a fictional universe where her entire

00:13:29.670 --> 00:13:34.310
tribe was full of savages, and she was the
one person who had hope of having a soul.

00:13:34.310 --> 00:13:40.200
He created a best-selling book based on lies,
stained with the blood of the innocent.

00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:44.370
Seething with rage, Pocahontas confronted
John Smith, and told him off for ripping her

00:13:44.370 --> 00:13:46.089
away from her family for the sake of his own
glory.

00:13:46.089 --> 00:13:48.449
She said, “Your countrymen will lie much.”

00:13:48.449 --> 00:13:56.720
Death, and Legacy
As we mentioned earlier, John Rolfe owned

00:13:56.720 --> 00:14:00.779
a tobacco farm in Virginia, but he lived in
England, and managed the incoming shipment

00:14:00.779 --> 00:14:01.779
of his product.

00:14:01.779 --> 00:14:05.180
Rolfe’s ships were going back and forth
to Virginia on a regular basis, and Pocahontas

00:14:05.180 --> 00:14:07.431
begged him to let her return home on one of
them.

00:14:07.431 --> 00:14:11.139
The better she got at speaking English, the
more dangerous it became for the men who captured

00:14:11.139 --> 00:14:12.600
her, and lied about it.

00:14:12.600 --> 00:14:17.129
As time went on, it became possible that she
would eventually expose the truth.

00:14:17.129 --> 00:14:22.019
So in 1617, when their son was 2 years old,
he finally agreed to bring them back to visit

00:14:22.019 --> 00:14:23.019
her family.

00:14:23.019 --> 00:14:28.230
Not long after they set sail, Pocahontas contracted
a mysterious illness, and died on the boat.

00:14:28.230 --> 00:14:31.959
While there is no record of what killed her,
most assumed that it must have been one of

00:14:31.959 --> 00:14:35.079
the many illnesses that the English had built
up an immunity to.

00:14:35.079 --> 00:14:38.839
At the time, no one dared to suggest that
it may have been foul play.

00:14:38.839 --> 00:14:42.319
When she died, they turned the ship back around,
and returned to England.

00:14:42.319 --> 00:14:45.930
Pocahontas was buried in St. George’s Church
in Gravesend.

00:14:45.930 --> 00:14:49.430
Rolfe could have afforded to give his wife
the best funeral money could buy, but she

00:14:49.430 --> 00:14:52.550
was given a pauper’s burial in an unmarked
grave.

00:14:52.550 --> 00:14:55.769
Young Thomas was sick with the same illness
that had killed his mother.

00:14:55.769 --> 00:14:59.220
Instead of helping to nurse his son back to
health and help him cope with the trauma of

00:14:59.220 --> 00:15:04.019
losing his mother, John Rolfe left him with
some random dude called Sir Lewis Stuckley,

00:15:04.019 --> 00:15:07.939
and sailed back to Virginia...Because if we
know anything in modern times, it’s that

00:15:07.939 --> 00:15:12.120
leaving kids to live with strange men on their
own has always worked out perfectly fine.

00:15:12.120 --> 00:15:13.120
Nope.

00:15:13.120 --> 00:15:14.120
Nothing bad has ever come of that.

00:15:14.120 --> 00:15:15.970
Thomas made a full recovery, but it was clear
that John Rolfe no longer wanted anything

00:15:15.970 --> 00:15:16.970
to do with him.

00:15:16.970 --> 00:15:21.269
In his mind, he knew that the boy probably
wasn’t his biological son, anyway, and it

00:15:21.269 --> 00:15:25.079
would seem as if he felt no fatherly love
towards him at all.

00:15:25.079 --> 00:15:28.889
John’s brother, Henry Rolfe, finally learned
that his nephew was being watched over by

00:15:28.889 --> 00:15:32.639
this Lewis Stuckley guy, and he quickly jumped
in to take custody of the boy.

00:15:32.639 --> 00:15:36.509
Henry was shocked by his brother’s cold
heartedness and willingness to abandon his

00:15:36.509 --> 00:15:38.550
own son with a complete stranger.

00:15:38.550 --> 00:15:42.720
Even though John Rolfe was wealthy from tobacco
farming, he refused to give any money to his

00:15:42.720 --> 00:15:44.189
brother for child support.

00:15:44.189 --> 00:15:49.160
Henry Rolfe actually had to sue John in court,
demanding that his nephew be left with something.

00:15:49.160 --> 00:15:53.059
John Rolfe never had any other descendents,
so when he died, Thomas inherited the tobacco

00:15:53.059 --> 00:15:55.279
farm in Virginia when he was 20 years old.

00:15:55.279 --> 00:15:58.449
This was the first time that Thomas Rolfe
had sailed back to Virginia from England.

00:15:58.449 --> 00:16:01.990
Now, he was a wealthy landowner, and he had
to learn about the tobacco farming business.

00:16:01.990 --> 00:16:04.870
He also became a lieutenant of the Jamestown
Colony.

00:16:04.870 --> 00:16:09.079
Thomas grew up hearing the stories of his
mother, Pocahontas, and he knew that his grandfather,

00:16:09.079 --> 00:16:12.769
half-sister, and his many aunts and uncles
would have been overjoyed to see him.

00:16:12.769 --> 00:16:16.160
By this time, there had been several wars
and conflicts between the Native Americans

00:16:16.160 --> 00:16:17.160
and the colonists.

00:16:17.160 --> 00:16:20.959
There were now strict rules that the colonists
should not interact with the Native Americans,

00:16:20.959 --> 00:16:22.000
and vice versa.

00:16:22.000 --> 00:16:25.949
Thomas Rolfe tried to petition the governor
of Virginia to let him meet his family, but

00:16:25.949 --> 00:16:27.709
his request was denied.

00:16:27.709 --> 00:16:29.680
He lived among the white colonists of Jamestown.

00:16:29.680 --> 00:16:34.559
If he ever found a way to speak to his relatives,
he would have had to do it in secret, and

00:16:34.559 --> 00:16:36.130
there would never be a record of it.

00:16:36.130 --> 00:16:40.999
Yes, this story was incredibly tragic and
downright infuriating.

00:16:40.999 --> 00:16:41.999
We wish there was a happy ending.

00:16:41.999 --> 00:16:45.499
Unfortunately, sometimes, the bad guys win.

00:16:45.499 --> 00:16:49.810
But if it were not for Pocahontas’ resilience
and strength, this story could have been so

00:16:49.810 --> 00:16:50.810
much worse.

00:16:50.810 --> 00:16:54.339
She was such a special person, that she was
able to prove to the English that the natives

00:16:54.339 --> 00:16:57.529
truly did have souls, and were worthy of maintaining
their culture.

00:16:57.529 --> 00:17:01.610
If there is any light in this dark story,
it is that Pocahontas became a well-loved

00:17:01.610 --> 00:17:03.560
icon for hundreds of years.

00:17:03.560 --> 00:17:08.159
For all of the historic inaccuracies, the
movies inspires a love and understanding of

00:17:08.159 --> 00:17:09.689
Native American culture, as well.

00:17:09.689 --> 00:17:14.230
Instead of focusing on the darkest parts of
her past, the world has chosen to celebrate

00:17:14.230 --> 00:17:18.179
her best qualities- Her friendliness, kindness,
and diplomacy.

00:17:18.179 --> 00:17:22.209
She chose to embrace the name she gave herself-
“the playful one”, because at the end

00:17:22.209 --> 00:17:25.040
of the day, it’s still who she truly was.

00:17:25.040 --> 00:17:29.010
We’re not going to ask if you enjoyed that
one, but feel free to vent your frustrations

00:17:29.010 --> 00:17:30.050
in the comments below.

00:17:30.050 --> 00:17:37.360
Or, if you’re trying to cheer up after hearing
that story, what Native American style name

00:17:37.360 --> 00:17:39.400
would you call yourself?

00:17:39.400 --> 00:17:40.929
Thanks for watching.

